'use strict';
(() => {
const journeys={
spinach:{icon:'🥬',name:'Spinach',intro:'A food label tells us what spinach contains. This journey shows why the body may not take in every bit.',steps:['Chewing breaks the thin leaves into small pieces.','In the small intestine, the body starts taking in iron, calcium, folate and orange-green plant pigments.','Spinach has a plant form of iron. Vitamin C in the same meal can help the body take in more of it.','A natural compound called oxalate can hold on to some calcium, so the body may take in less than the label shows.','A little fat in the meal can help the body take in some plant pigments.','Fiber moves on to the large intestine, where helpful gut bacteria can use some of it.'],more:'The main idea is simple: the amount in food and the amount the body can use are not always the same. Cooking and the other foods in a meal can change the journey.'},
banana:{icon:'🍌',name:'Banana',intro:'A banana changes before you eat it, especially as it turns from green to yellow.',steps:['A green banana holds more of its carbohydrate as starch.','As it ripens, natural enzymes change some starch into sugar and make the fruit softer.','Chewing mixes the soft fruit with saliva.','The small intestine breaks down starch and takes in simple sugars.','Cells can use the sugar for energy, and the liver and muscles can store some for later.','Fiber and some starch move on to the large intestine, where gut bacteria can use part of them.'],more:'A green banana and a very ripe banana are not exactly the same. The amount eaten and the rest of the meal also matter.'},
apple:{icon:'🍎',name:'Apple',intro:'Apple sugar, water and fiber do not all follow the same path.',steps:['Chewing breaks the crisp flesh and skin into small pieces.','The small intestine takes in the apple’s simple sugars.','Water is taken in along the digestive tract.','Pectin and other fibers are not fully broken down, so they travel farther.','In the large intestine, gut bacteria can use some of the pectin.','The skin adds fiber and plant compounds, so a peeled apple is a little different from a whole apple.'],more:'Filtered apple juice loses much of the whole fruit’s fiber and needs less chewing. That is why juice and a whole apple are not the same experience.'},
orange:{icon:'🍊',name:'Orange',intro:'Orange shows how vitamin C and pulp can play different roles.',steps:['Biting the segments releases juice but leaves some pulp and thin walls to chew.','Vitamin C mixes with the watery part of digestion.','The small intestine takes it in, and the body uses it for jobs such as making collagen.','Vitamin C in a meal can help the body take in more iron from plant foods.','The pulp and segment walls add fiber that filtered juice mostly removes.','The body controls vitamin C levels and removes extra amounts it does not keep.'],more:'A whole orange has more of its natural fiber than filtered juice. It also shows how one food can help the body use a nutrient from another food.'},
carrot:{icon:'🥕',name:'Carrot',intro:'A carrot’s orange color comes mainly from beta-carotene, which the body can turn into vitamin A.',steps:['Chewing breaks the firm carrot into small pieces.','Cooking can soften plant cells and release more beta-carotene.','In the small intestine, bile and fat help carry beta-carotene.','Some beta-carotene then moves into the cells of the intestine.','The body can turn part of it into vitamin A when needed.','Fiber that is not broken down continues to the large intestine.'],more:'Raw and cooked carrots can both fit a healthy diet. A little fat in the meal can help the body take in beta-carotene, but carrots do not give “super vision.”'},
guava:{icon:'🍈',name:'Guava',intro:'Guava has plenty of vitamin C and fiber, and each follows a different path.',steps:['Chewing breaks the flesh and seeds into smaller pieces.','Sugar and vitamin C separate from the fiber during digestion.','The small intestine takes in vitamin C.','The body uses vitamin C for several jobs, but it does not store an endless amount.','Much of the fiber moves past the small intestine.','In the large intestine, bacteria use some fiber and the rest helps add bulk to stool.'],more:'Guava can provide a lot of vitamin C, but one nutrient does not tell the whole story. The amount eaten, variety and ripeness also matter.'},
avocado:{icon:'🥑',name:'Avocado',intro:'Avocado contains more fat than most fruits, so its journey is a little different.',steps:['Chewing breaks the creamy flesh and releases small drops of fat.','Fat can make a meal leave the stomach more slowly.','Bile enters the small intestine and helps split fat into tiny drops.','Digestive enzymes break the fat into smaller parts.','Intestine cells take in those parts and pack them for travel through the body.','Avocado fiber continues to the large intestine, where bacteria can use some of it.'],more:'Avocado has more energy than watery fruits because fat gives more energy per gram. Its fat can also help the body take in some plant pigments from other vegetables.'},
broccoli:{icon:'🥦',name:'Broccoli',intro:'Chopping and cooking can change broccoli before digestion even begins.',steps:['Chopping or chewing breaks open broccoli cells and lets natural plant chemicals mix.','These chemicals can change into new compounds. Strong heat lowers the action of some plant enzymes.','Vitamin C and folate mix with water during digestion. Some orange-green pigments are easier to take in after they leave the plant cells.','Long boiling can move some nutrients into the cooking water. Steaming uses less water.','Broccoli fiber reaches the large intestine because the body cannot fully break it down.','Helpful gut bacteria can use some of that fiber.'],more:'Raw and cooked broccoli can both be useful foods. Cooking changes texture and nutrients in different ways, so one method is not always best.'},
cucumber:{icon:'🥒',name:'Cucumber',intro:'Cucumber shows how water and fiber can travel in different ways.',steps:['Chewing breaks the crisp cells and releases much of their water.','The stomach mixes the pieces with the rest of the meal.','Water is taken in through the digestive tract and joins the body’s normal fluid balance.','Small amounts of vitamins and minerals are taken in mainly through the small intestine.','The skin and seeds add fiber that is not fully broken down.','That fiber moves to the large intestine and helps add bulk to stool.'],more:'Cucumber contains a lot of water, but it does not replace every drink or meet everyone’s fluid needs by itself.'},
tomato:{icon:'🍅',name:'Tomato',intro:'Tomato carries water, vitamin C and a red plant pigment called lycopene.',steps:['Chewing breaks the skin and soft flesh.','Water and vitamin C mix with the watery part of digestion.','The small intestine takes in vitamin C and other small nutrients.','Lycopene must first leave the plant cells before the body can take it in.','Cooking can help release some lycopene, and a little fat in the meal can help the body take it in.','Skin and seeds add fiber that continues to the large intestine.'],more:'Raw and cooked tomato are both useful. Heat may lower some vitamin C while making some lycopene easier for the body to use.'},
sweetpotato:{icon:'🍠',name:'Sweet potato',intro:'Sweet potato gives the body starch, fiber and orange beta-carotene.',steps:['Chewing breaks the cooked root into smaller pieces.','Digestive juices break much of its starch into simple sugar.','The small intestine takes in that sugar, which cells can use for energy.','Orange beta-carotene leaves the softened plant cells.','A little fat in the meal can help the body take in beta-carotene.','Fiber moves on to the large intestine and supports normal bowel movement.'],more:'Cooking makes sweet potato softer and helps release some beta-carotene. Added sugar or deep frying changes the whole meal, not just the vegetable.'},
lentils:{icon:'🫘',name:'Lentils',intro:'Lentils bring together plant protein, starch, iron and plenty of fiber.',steps:['Cooking softens dry lentils so they are safe and easier to chew.','In the stomach and small intestine, enzymes start breaking down protein and starch.','Amino acids from protein and sugar from starch can then be taken in.','Lentil iron is a plant form. Vitamin C in the same meal can help the body take in more.','Some starch and fiber are not fully broken down in the small intestine.','Gut bacteria use part of that material, which can also make gas.'],more:'Start with a comfortable amount if you are not used to beans or lentils. Rinsing canned lentils can also lower some of the added salt.'}
};

const ui=Object.fromEntries(['foodList','title','intro','timeline','closer'].map(id=>[id,document.getElementById(id)]));
function readSelection(){const food=new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('food');return Object.hasOwn(journeys,food)?food:'spinach';}
let selected=readSelection();
ui.foodList.innerHTML=Object.entries(journeys).map(([id,j])=>'<button data-id="'+id+'">'+j.icon+' '+j.name+'</button>').join('');
function render(){
 const j=journeys[selected];
 ui.foodList.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(b=>{b.classList.toggle('active',b.dataset.id===selected);b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(b.dataset.id===selected));});
 ui.title.textContent=j.icon+' '+j.name+'’s journey';ui.intro.textContent=j.intro;
 ui.timeline.innerHTML=j.steps.map((s,i)=>'<div class="step"><b>'+(i+1)+'</b><div>'+s+'</div></div>').join('');
 ui.closer.innerHTML='<strong>Look closer</strong><br>'+j.more;
}
ui.foodList.addEventListener('click',e=>{
 const b=e.target.closest('[data-id]');if(!b)return;selected=b.dataset.id;
 const url=new URL(location.href);url.searchParams.set('food',selected);url.hash='journey-detail';
 history.replaceState(null,'',url);render();
});
window.addEventListener('popstate',()=>{selected=readSelection();render();});
render();
})();
